[:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/65154"} "32 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/65675"} "Stefka Kostadinova"] " had been the world’s top high jumper since 1986 but had failed badly in both 1988 and 1992 in her attempt to win Olympic gold. She had set three world records, and her mark of 2.09 (6-10¼) from 1987 still stood (and stands thru 2019), and had cleared 2.05 metres (6-8¾) 20 times, a mark cleared only five times by other women. She was World Champion in 1987 and 1995. Thru 2.01 (6-7) metres in the final round, three jumpers survived – Kostadinova, " [:a {:href "/athletes/77099"} "Inha Babakova"] " (UKR), and Greece’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/70683"} "Niki Bakogianni"] " – but Kostadinova and Babakova led as they were perfect to that point. But Babakova went out at 2.03 (6-7¾), while Bakogianni had a surprise clearance at that height on her third attempt. However, Kostadinova still led, and was still perfect. She cemented the gold medal by jumping 2.05 metres (6-8¾)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3110"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/65675"} "Stefka Kostadinova"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BUL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BUL.png"}] "BUL"]] [:td {} "1.93 (=1)"] [:td {} "2.05 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3387"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/70683"} "Niki Bakogianni"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedi